-## Subtopics
-
-### DAVIGS
-Uses differentiable volume rendering with Gaussian primitives but focuses on differentiable volume rendering techniques. Choose when volumetric accuracy is prioritized over rasterization speed. [DAVIGS](12021-davigs.md)
-
-### Spherical Harmonics Optimization
-Optimizes SH coefficients directly for view-dependent color. Choose for simpler view-dependent effects without explicit lighting models. [Spherical Harmonics Optimization](14206-spherical-harmonics-optimization.md)
-
-### SWAG
-Appearance-conditioned Gaussians for in-the-wild images. Choose when handling varying appearance conditions (lighting, weather) in uncontrolled environments. [SWAG](14315-swag-splatting-in-the-wild-images-with-appearance-conditione.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Grid Boundary:** If the illumination grid is not shrunk midway through training, it may cover empty space, wasting memory and potentially causing interpolation artifacts at the boundaries [S1].
+*   **Initialization:** The initial boundary must encapsulate the scene bounding box to ensure all Gaussians are queried correctly during the early training phase [S1].
+*   **Precision:** The factorized tensor representation relies on accurate interpolation; ensure the interpolation method (e.g., trilinear) is consistent with the factorization scheme [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed is comparable to 3DGS due to rasterization and pre-calculated illumination field [S1]. Training involves neural network evaluation per Gaussian, adding overhead compared to base 3DGS.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The illumination grid uses $150^3$ voxels, which is 87.5% less than TensoRF on synthetic datasets [S1]. Memory footprint is low compared to the number of optimized Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Performance:** On the Shiny Blender dataset, 3iGS achieves PSNR 27.51, SSIM 0.930, LPIPS 0.045 [S1]. It surpasses 3DGS in scenes with reflective surfaces and specular highlights [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Accurate specular view-dependent effects, fast rendering, memory-efficient illumination field.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires neural network evaluation for specular shading, potentially slower training than base 3DGS. Not given in the sources for specific hardware benchmarks.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed is comparable to 3DGS because illumination is pre-calculated and stored in the grid, avoiding per-ray tracing [S1]. Training involves optimizing the grid and BRDF features, which adds overhead compared to base 3DGS but remains efficient due to the compact tensor representation [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The illumination field uses $150^3$ voxels, which is 87.5% less than TensoRF on synthetic datasets [S1]. The memory footprint is low compared to the number of optimized Gaussians [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the Shiny Blender dataset, 3iGS achieves PSNR 27.51, SSIM 0.930, and LPIPS 0.045 [S1]. It outperforms 3DGS in scenes with reflective surfaces [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Accurate modeling of specular view-dependent effects; fast rendering; compact memory usage [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of grid boundary and training phases; may not generalize as well to unseen lighting conditions as methods using explicit light probes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3iGS was introduced by Z.J. Tang and T.J. Cham [S1]. It addresses the limitations of 3DGS in modeling specular effects and view-dependent colors by incorporating a local illumination field and BRDF features [S1]. It builds on the 3DGS rasterization pipeline and TensoRF's factorised tensor representation [S1].
+3iGS was introduced by Z.J. Tang and T.J. Cham [S1]. It builds on the limitations of 3DGS, which optimizes outgoing radiance in isolation without accounting for scene lighting or adjacent Gaussians [S1]. It differs from GaussianShader [13], which uses global environment lights and SH-based color, by using local grid-based tensors for illumination [S1].
 
